<section class="case-summary"><div><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>A falling metric had several plausible explanations—and no single view showed the whole system.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>This self-initiated example follows a customer-satisfaction scenario from performance decline through intervention and recovery. It is designed for contexts where results, decisions and external pressures are being interpreted differently by different people.</p><p>Instead of forcing every question into one complex diagram, the sequence gives each visual tool a distinct job.</p></div></section>

<section class="decision-band"><span>02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The decision</p><h2>Move from the most accessible view to the deepest one.</h2></div></section>

<section class="case-gallery">
  <figure class="gallery-wide"><img src="/img/portfolio/linea-csat-performance-recovery-timeline.webp" width="2400" height="1350" loading="lazy" alt="Linea timeline chart showing a fall and recovery in customer satisfaction."><figcaption><strong>What happened?</strong> The annotated timeline shows the fall, lowest point and recovery alongside operational change.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/portfolio/linea-training-investment-lag-timeline.webp" width="2400" height="1350" loading="lazy" alt="Linea timeline showing the delay between training investment and performance recovery."><figcaption><strong>Why were results delayed?</strong> Separate decision and action lines show that work was underway before the metric recovered.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/portfolio/linea-csat-causal-loop-system-diagram.webp" width="2400" height="1350" loading="lazy" alt="Linea causal loop diagram explaining interactions behind customer satisfaction performance."><figcaption><strong>How did the parts interact?</strong> A causal loop map replaces a single-cause story with a system explanation.</figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section class="case-outcome"><div><p class="eyebrow">The practical output</p><h2>A presentation sequence that supports both general and specialist conversations.</h2></div><p>This is self-initiated work, created to demonstrate our <a href="/services/information-design/">information design</a> approach to structure and mechanism. Simpler views can stand alone; the causal model is available when a guided, deeper discussion is needed.</p></section>

